  "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(s+2)$ is $s\\in (-2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component ${e}^{2s}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\frac{3}{{s}^{2}}$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/7aacfa509e2e642fd90ef989e4dec3a2f9f5ad1e8def55f894b88d97bcabb971.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is $s\\in (-2,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
